/-!
# Gerasimova–Osin, Proposition 4.1, from Lemma 4.2

Gerasimova–Osin (arXiv:1910.14524v3), §4:

> **Proposition 4.1.** Let `G` be an acylindrically hyperbolic group with trivial finite
> radical, `F` a non-empty finite subset of `G`.  Then there exists a length function `ℓ` on
> `G`, an element `t ∈ G`, and a symmetric `G`-equivariant generalized combing
> `C : G × G → 𝒫(G)` satisfying the following conditions.  (a) The set `tF` freely generates
> a free subsemigroup `S` of `G`.  (b) `C(1, s) ∩ C(s, g) ∩ C(1, g) ≠ ∅` for all `g ∈ G` and
> `s ∈ S`.  (c) The associated growth functions `γ` and `ρ` computed with respect to the
> length function `ℓ` are bounded by a linear function from above.
>
> … it suffices to prove the proposition for a shift `gF` for some `g ∈ G`.  Since `F` is
> finite, there is an element `g ∈ G ∖ F⁻¹`.  Clearly `1 ∉ gF` for such `g`.  Thus we can
> assume that `1 ∉ F` (12) without loss of generality.

## Repository form

`gerasimovaOsinProposition41_of_lemma42` assembles Lemmas 4.3–4.8 over the two-sided
Lemma 4.2 carried by `GerasimovaOsinLemma42Statement`:

* Proposition 3.6 is `dgoProposition414Uniform` at `(1,1)`, both clauses;
* (16) is `exists_deep_letter` at radius `50C`, `H = ⟨y⟩` being infinite;
* `F ∩ H = ∅` is (13) at `f = g = h`;
* (a) is `gerasimovaOsinLemma45`, (b) is `gerasimovaOsinLemma48`, symmetry and equivariance
  are Lemma 4.6, and (22)–(23) are Lemma 4.7, with `ℓ = |·|_𝒜`.

The shift `F ↦ g₀F` uses `t g₀` for `t`: `(t g₀)F = t(g₀F)`.  An element `g₀ ∉ F⁻¹` exists
because `G` is infinite, which Lemma 4.2 at `F = ∅` shows (it gives an element of infinite
order).

## Manuscript status

Infrastructure for `cor:regular-nonmf-algebra`: with
`gerasimovaOsinTheorem11Printed_of_proposition41` it reduces
`GerasimovaOsinTheorem11Printed` to `GerasimovaOsinLemma42Statement`.
-/

